==English==
{{was wotd|2010|March|18}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|la|en}} {{term|friabilis|friābilis|lang=la}}, from {{term|frio|friō|to crumble|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|frī'ə-bəl}}, {{IPA|/ˈfɹaɪəbl̩/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|aɪəbəl}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Easily broken into small [[fragment]]s, [[crumble]]d, or reduced to [[powder]].
#* '''1977''', {{w|Angela Carter}}, ''The Passion of New Eve'':
#*: Spiders had woven their vague trapezes between the '''friable''' heads of dead peonies in enormous glass jars streaked with tide marks where the water had evaporated long ago.
#* '''1983''', {{w|Lawrence Durrell}}, ''Sebastian'', Faber & Faber 2004 (''Avignon Quintet''), p. 1020:
#*: This light, '''friable''' type of material offered excellent insulation against both desert heat and also the cold of darkness during the winter.
# {{context|of soil}} [[loose|Loose]] and large-grained in [[consistency]].
# {{context|of [[poison|poisons]]}} Likely to crumble and become [[airborne]], thus becoming a [[health]] [[risk]]
#*: '''April 1987''', ''Old-House Journal''
#*: It is when [[asbestos]]-containing products are '''friable''' that hazardous asbestos fibers are likely to be released and sent airborne.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|easily broken into small fragments}} [[crumbly]]

